  "narrative": {
    "intro": "North Carolina has $3.5B in federal contract obligations for FY2026 to date. The Department of Defense obligated $1.5B, making the Department of Defense the largest buying agency in the state so far this fiscal year. Obligations fell 63.0% year over year. Federal buyers issued 135,596 awards, with an average award size of $25.5K. FY2026 to date is a partial fiscal-year total and follows a five-year series that peaked at $9.3B in FY25 and ranged from $6.7B to $8.6B in earlier years. Computer Systems Design Services is the largest industry by obligations at $317M. GENERAL DYNAMICS INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY, INC. is the top vendor with $174M in obligations. The set of top agencies and top vendors shows a mix of defense, civilian agencies, large IT contractors, and facility and service contractors active in North Carolina this fiscal year.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How does FY2026 to date compare with prior fiscal years?",
        "body": "FY2026 to date shows $3.5B in obligations versus $9.3B in FY25. Obligations fell 63.0% year over year. The five-year series includes $6.7B in FY21, $8.6B in FY22, $7B in FY23, and $7.2B in FY24, which provides context for the current partial-year total."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which agencies and vendors dominate federal contracting in North Carolina?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense is the largest buyer with $1.5B in obligations. The General Services Administration and the Department of Veterans Affairs follow with $487M and $447M respectively. GENERAL DYNAMICS INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY, INC. is the largest vendor at $174M, with SDV OFFICE SYSTEMS LLC and RESEARCH TRIANGLE INSTITUTE next at $153M and $148M respectively."
      }
    ]
  },
